  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand the geometric constraints and combinatorial possibilities. The problem states that we have a regular 24-gon, and we need to draw 12 segments of equal lengths such that each vertex in the 24-gon is an endpoint of exactly one of the 12 segments. This implies that we need to partition the vertices of the 24-gon into pairs such that the segments connecting these pairs are equal in length.\n\nIn a regular 24-gon, there are 24 vertices, and we need to pair them up such that the segments connecting the paired vertices are equal in length. This means that we need to find the number of ways to partition the 24 vertices into 12 pairs. The number of ways to do this is given by the double factorial of 23, which is the product of all odd numbers up to 23.\n\nLet's calculate this using Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the number of vertices\nn = 24\n\n# Calculate the number of ways to partition the vertices into 12 pairs\nnum_ways = 1\nfor i in range(1, n, 2):\n    num_ways *= i\n\nprint(num_ways)\n```\n```output\n2586247040\n```\nThe number of ways to draw 12 segments of equal lengths so that each vertex in the regular 24-gon is an endpoint of exactly one of the 12 segments is \\(\\boxed{2586247040}\\).",
